    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm seeing some weird damage on my tomato plants in the greenhouse. there are these tiny, irregular holes appearing on the leaves, and it looks like something is eating them from the underside. i cant really see anything crawling on them during the day. any idea what might be causing this?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Tiny, irregular holes on the underside of tomato leaves, especially when you can't spot the culprits during the day, often point to some small, stealthy pests. It sounds like you might be dealing with **spider mites** or possibly **thrips**. These little pests are often harder to see than a big slug, and they can cause damage that looks like tiny holes or stippling on the leaves.\n\nYou should check the undersides of several leaves thoroughly, perhaps under a bright lamp, in the early morning or late evening when they might be more active. If you see fine webbing, it's almost certainly spider mites, and you'll need to treat them immediately with insecticidal soap or neem oil. Good luck with your tomatoes!",
