    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for that. i'll look for webbing. i also noticed some of the stems look a bit nibbled too, almost like something was just gnawing on them overnight. it's really frustrating because i've been so careful with the greenhouse security and everything. it just feels like there are these tiny little intruders sneaking in when the lights go out. any other possibilities besides mites?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Nibbled stems, especially overnight, bring a different set of possibilities to mind, and it sounds like you might be dealing with a different kind of hungry little creature. While spider mites are notorious leaf-eaters, stem damage overnight suggests something that might be more actively feeding or burrowing. You could be dealing with **aphids** that are congregating on the stems, or even very small **cutworms** that might be getting into the greenhouse if there are any gaps.\n\nIf the gnawing is truly localized and on the stems, consider if any small, sap-sucking insects are hiding in the crevices, or if any tiny, nocturnal larvae are present. Sometimes, even small amounts of moisture or a slight gap in your netting can allow a small, hungry pest to slip through. It's a frustrating situation, but you can track it down!",
      "source": "target",
